<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App Sandbox entitlements for the main OmniDB executable and the outer
  .app bundle (see Makefile's _build_mac target) — used whether it's
  currently ad-hoc-signed for direct/Homebrew distribution or, later,
  signed with a real Apple Distribution identity for the Mac App Store.
  Same binary, same sandbox either way; only the signing identity
  (MAC_SIGN_IDENTITY) and the extra App Store submission step (a
  productbuild .pkg, not part of this Makefile yet) change later.

  NOT used for omnidb-server, the second executable bundled in the same
  Contents/MacOS/ — that one is signed with entitlements-helper.plist
  instead. Giving it this same full entitlement set crashes it instantly on
  launch (see that file's comment for the full story); it doesn't need its
  own copy of any of these anyway, since com.apple.security.inherit already
  gives it everything the sandbox this file establishes already allows.

  Why each entitlement is needed:
   - app-sandbox: mandatory for every Mac App Store submission.
   - cs.allow-jit: without this, the window renders and nothing else —
     confirmed by hand: WKWebView's WebContent helper process fails to
     initialize (Console showed repeated "sandbox does not allow access to
     RunningBoard" / XPC bootstrap failures to launchservicesd,
     coreservicesd, etc.) and the whole window just stays blank forever,
     with no error and no console output anywhere. JavaScriptCore needs to
     JIT-compile, and Hardened Runtime blocks that without this entitlement
     — every other symptom traced back to this one missing key.
   - network.client: every database engine OmniDB connects to (Postgres,
     MySQL, Oracle, MSSQL, Firebird, SQLite is local-file-only) and its SSH
     tunnel/terminal feature (ssh_tunnel.go, terminal.go) are outbound TCP
     connections to hosts the user configures — made from omnidb-server,
     which needs this via inheritance (see above), not its own declaration.
   - network.server: omnidb-server (go-server/listen_addr.go) and this
     executable's own save-dialog/open-url relay (wails-app/savedialog.go)
     each bind a 127.0.0.1-only TCP listener. Sandbox requires this
     entitlement even for loopback-only listeners.
   - files.user-selected.read-write: the native Save/Open panels
     (wails-app/savedialog.go, wails-app/legacydata.go) need write access to
     wherever the user picks, and read access to whatever legacy data
     folder they pick during the one-time import flow.
   - files.bookmarks.app-scope: lets a security-scoped bookmark to a
     user-picked file be persisted across launches, so a location the user
     chose once keeps working instead of being re-prompted for on every
     relaunch. Relied on by the .pgpass grant (wails-app/pgpassdialog.go):
     without it, the bookmark this app saves would still encode and resolve
     fine but carry no working sandbox grant, and every launch would ask
     the user to point at their .pgpass file again.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
